BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20261027T170000Z
DTEND:20261027T181500Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:EASC Special Event: A Conversation on Future Career Paths with Fo
 rmer U.S. Senior Diplomat Sarah Beran
DESCRIPTION:Join the East Asian Studies Center\, 21st Century Japan Polit
 ics and Society Initiative\, and the Hamilton Lugar Office for Policy Eng
 agement and Strategic Partnerships on Tuesday October 27 for an informal 
 lunch and career conversation with special guest Sarah Beran\, Incoming P
 resident of the National Committee on U.S.-China Relations.Sarah Beran sp
 ent 23 years in the U.S. Foreign Service\, most recently as Deputy Chief 
 of Mission at the U.S. Embassy in Beijing. Before that\, she was the Seni
 or Director for China and Taiwan Affairs at the White House National Secu
 rity Council.This lunch is for graduate and undergraduate students - atte
 ndance is capped at the first 25 students to RSVP.&#160\;Sarah Beran biog
 raphy: Sarah Beran is president-elect of the National Committee on U.S.-C
 hina Relations. She previously served as a partner at Macro Advisory Part
 ners\, which she joined in 2025 after a distinguished career in the U.S. 
 Foreign Service — most recently as deputy chief of mission at the U.S. 
 Embassy in Beijing.  From 2022 to 2024\, Ms. Beran served as senior
  director for China and Taiwan Affairs at the White House National Se
 curity Council. Her portfolio encompassed technology export controls\, in
 vestment screening\, trade policy\, counternarcotics\, Russia sanctions a
 nd Taiwan contingency planning. She led strategic preparations for multip
 le heads-of-state summits\, negotiated the reopening of senior diplomatic
  channels with Beijing\, and helped forge the first U.S.-China understand
 ing on AI safety in the context of nuclear command and control.   Ms. B
 eran also served as then-U.S. Secretary of State Antony Blinken's dep
 uty executive secretary for the Indo-Pacific\, led the office respons
 ible for U.S. engagement in APEC\, and served as former U.S. Secret
 ary of State Mike Pompeo's director of the office of Chinese and Mo
 ngolian Affairs. She was posted overseas in Beijing\, Islamabad\, Jerusal
 em\, and Quito. Her previous domestic assignments include office 
 director for economic policy in the Bureau of East Asian and Pacific 
 Affairs\, special assistant to then-Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice\,
  and Lebanon desk officer.   Ms. Beran speaks Mandarin and Spanish.
  She joined the Foreign Service in 2002 and graduated from Claremon
 t McKenna College in 1999.
