 ts.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/308/crop/1/src_region/131\,
 156\,672\,712/32137_CRey_photo.rev.1787346934.jpg" title="CRey photo" dat
 a-max-w="541" data-max-h="556" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></pi
 cture></p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><strong>Bio</strong></p><p>  Dr. Camilo Rey-
 Sabogal is an Associate Professor at the Faculty of Social Sciences and E
 ducation\, Universidad de Cartagena\, Colombia. He investigates the polit
 ical economy of urban space\, focusing on how territorial transformations
  shape access to housing\, work\, and rights for vulnerable populations. 
 His research examines residential segregation\, migration\, gentrificatio
 n\, housing policy and urban inequalities\, with sustained fieldwork in C
 artagena and the Colombian Caribbean.</p><p><strong>Title</strong></p><p>
   Land Scarcity and Flood-Risk Zoning: How Tourism Gentrification and Cli
 mate Change Limit Affordable Housing in Cartagena</p><p><strong>Abstract<
 /strong></p><p>  Over the past two decades\, Cartagena has undergone majo
 r housing market transformations driven by tourism-driven real estate spe
 culation\, the financialization of housing\, climate-driven flooding risk
 s\, and migration pressures. This research examines how tourism gentrific
 ation and the reduction of buildable land due to flood risk jointly const
 rain affordable housing production in Cartagena\, and identifies institut
 ional arrangements capable of improving the governance of scarce\, secure
  urban land under conditions of real estate pressure and climate vulnerab
 ility.</p><p>  The study employs a mixed-methods approach combining quant
 itative spatial analysis of housing price dynamics across urban submarket
 s\, multi-layered vulnerability mapping\, and institutional analysis of p
 lanning mechanisms. It addresses three core questions: (1) How do tourism
 -driven gentrification dynamics generate ripple effects across housing su
 bmarkets that reduce the availability and affordability of social housing
 ? (2) How do climate-related flood risks exacerbate land scarcity and aff
 ect vulnerable households' access to housing? (3) What institutional arra
 ngements and governance mechanisms could reduce the social housing defici
 t in contexts shaped by real estate pressure and climate risk?</p><p><str
 ong>About the Ostrom Workshop Research Series</strong></p><p>  Learn abou
 t innovative research by Graduate Students and Visiting Scholars as they 
 advance the mission of the Ostrom Workshop\, building upon the theme of g
 overnance to understand and address major societal problems.</p>
