BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20261029T210000Z
DTEND:20261029T220000Z
LOCATION:355 north eagleson avenue
GEO:39.170057;-86.517303
SUMMARY:On Antarctic Law- Meet the Scholar: Jeffrey McGee
DESCRIPTION:Professor Jeff McGee focusses on international law and govern
 ance issues relating to Antarctica\, Oceans and Climate Change. As an int
 ernational lawyer with expertise also in global governance and geopolitic
 s\, he divides his time between the School of Law and the Institute for M
 arine and Antarctic Studies. Jeff is also Director of the Australian Foru
 m for Climate Intervention Governance (AFCIG)\, the leading national rese
 arch group on the governance of climate intervention technologies.ABOUT E
 VENT:  Raising new questions about how the Antarctic Treaty's non-militar
 ization obligations apply\, and how they can be modernized in circumstanc
 es where technologies being used in the region are inherently 'dual use'.
   There is a growing presence of space-related infrastructure in Antarcti
 ca\, such as satellite ground receiving stations\, telescopes\, autonomou
 s systems\, and their associated data networks. While these technologies 
 are important for modern scientific research and environmental monitoring
 \, many also possess inherent capabilities that could be incorporated int
 o military systems operating either within or beyond the Antarctic region
 .WHEN:Thursday\, October 29th from 5-6pm  WHERE:Hamilton Lugar School GA 
 1060.&#160\;  BIOGRAPHY:  Before joining the University of Tasmania in Ja
 nuary 2015\, Jeff was a Research Director\, Program Convenor and Senior L
 ecturer and at the Newcastle Law School. Prior to entering academic life 
 in 2008\, Jeff gained extensive practical legal experience through workin
 g as a senior lawyer with the Department of Defence and a decade of priva
 te practice as a partner in a leading law firm in New South Wales.  His w
 ork is widely published in leading international journals in the fields o
 f Antarctic governance and policy\, international environmental law\, glo
 bal environmental governance and climate change policy.  &#160\;  Refresh
 ments &amp\; snacks will be served.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<div data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><img
  src="https://i1.rgstatic.net/ii/profile.image/273692752084992-1442264845
 371_Q512/Jeffrey-Mcgee-4.jpg" alt="Jeffrey MCGEE | Senior Lecturer in Cli
 mate Change\, Marine and Antarctic ..." loading="lazy"/></div><div data-o
 lk-copy-source="MessageBody"><img src="https://www.utas.edu.au/__data/ass
 ets/git_bridge/0012/1368768/dist/mysource_files/logo.svg" alt="University
  of Tasmania logo" loading="lazy"/></div><div data-olk-copy-source="Messa
 geBody"></div><div data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"></div><p style="tex
 t-align: left\;" data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">  Professor Jeff McGe
 e focusses on international law and governance issues relating to Antarct
 ica\, Oceans and Climate Change. As an international lawyer with expertis
 e also in global governance and geopolitics\, he divides his time between
  the School of Law and the Institute for Marine and Antarctic Studies. Je
 ff is also Director of the Australian Forum for Climate Intervention Gove
 rnance (AFCIG)\, the leading national research group on the governance of
  climate intervention technologies.</p><div data-olk-copy-source="Message
 Body"></div><div data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><strong>ABOUT EVENT:<
 /strong></div><p data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">  Raising new questio
 ns about how the Antarctic Treaty's non-militarization obligations apply\
 , and how they can be modernized in circumstances where technologies bein
 g used in the region are inherently 'dual use'.</p><p data-olk-copy-sourc
 e="MessageBody">  There is a growing presence of space-related infrastruc
 ture in Antarctica\, such as satellite ground receiving stations\, telesc
 opes\, autonomous systems\, and their associated data networks. While the
 se technologies are important for modern scientific research and environm
 ental monitoring\, many also possess inherent capabilities that could be 
 incorporated into military systems operating either within or beyond the 
 Antarctic region.</p><div data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"></div><div d
 ata-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><strong>WHEN:</strong></div><ul><li dat
 a-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">Thursday\, October 29<sup>th</sup> from 5
 -6pm  </li></ul><div data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"></div><div data-o
 lk-copy-source="MessageBody"><strong>WHERE:</strong></div><ul><li data-ol
 k-copy-source="MessageBody">Hamilton Lugar School GA 1060.&#160\;  </li><
 /ul><p><strong>BIOGRAPHY:</strong><br />  Before joining the University o
 f Tasmania in January 2015\, Jeff was a Research Director\, Program Conve
 nor and Senior Lecturer and at the Newcastle Law School. Prior to enterin
 g academic life in 2008\, Jeff gained extensive practical legal experienc
 e through working as a senior lawyer with the Department of Defence and a
  decade of private practice as a partner in a leading law firm in New Sou
 th Wales.</p><p>  His work is widely published in leading international j
 ournals in the fields of Antarctic governance and policy\, international 
 environmental law\, global environmental governance and climate change po
 licy.</p><p>  &#160\;</p><p>  Refreshments &amp\; snacks will be served.<
 /p>
