LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
SUMMARY:Polar Governance Working Group: Jeffrey McGee
DESCRIPTION:The Antarctic Treaty\, Dual Use Technologies\, and the Challe
 nges of Non-Militarization  The Antarctic Treaty represents one of the mo
 st enduring and successful arms control agreements of the post–Second W
 orld War period. Concluded in 1959 at the height of early Cold War rivalr
 y\, the Treaty pioneered peaceful use of an entire continent\, by prohibi
 ting military bases\, military maneuvers\, and weapons testing - while pe
 rmitting the use of military personnel and equipment for scientific or ot
 her peaceful purposes. Central to this achievement is the non-militarizat
 ion obligation in the Treaty's Article I\, which is reinforced by an open
  onsite and aerial inspection regime.  This seminar examines how contempo
 rary technological developments are impacting these foundations. It highl
 ights the growing presence of space-related infrastructure in Antarctica\
 , such as satellite ground receiving stations\, telescopes\, autonomous s
 ystems\, and their associated data networks. While these technologies are
  important for modern scientific research and environmental monitoring\, 
 many also possess inherent capabilities that could be incorporated into m
 ilitary systems operating either within or beyond the Antarctic region. T
 his raises new questions about how the Treaty's non-militarization obliga
 tions apply\, and how they can be modernized in circumstances where techn
 ologies being used in the region are inherently 'dual use'.Bio  Professor
  Jeff McGee is focused on strategies that can help us govern Antarctica a
 nd the Southern Ocean. He is an expert in international law\, climate cha
 nge law and global environmental governance. Associate Professor McGee an
 alyses the strategies and structures of global and regional institutions 
 that seek to solve problems like climate change and the international law
 s that affect them. He provides critical analysis and commentary on the d
 evelopment and policy of institutions such as the Antarctic Treaty System
  and United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCC).  Prof
 essor Jeff McGee focusses on international law and governance issues rela
 ting to Antarctica\, Oceans and Climate Change. As an international lawye
 r with expertise also in global governance and geopolitics\, he divides h
 is time between the School of Law and the Institute for Marine and Antarc
 tic Studies. Jeff is also Director of the Australian Forum for Climate In
 tervention Governance (AFCIG)\, the leading national research group on th
 e governance of climate intervention technologies.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<picture class="lw_image lw_image32951  lw_a
 lign_left lw_block"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.e
 du/live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/300/32951_JMcgee.rev.1791464138.we
 bp 1x" data-origin="responsive"/><source type="image/jpeg" srcset="https:
 //events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/300/32951_JMcgee.rev.
 1791464138.jpg 1x" data-origin="responsive"/><img width="300" height="300
 " alt="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src="https://events.iu.edu/li
 ve/image/gid/105/width/300/height/300/32951_JMcgee.rev.1791464138.jpg" da
 ta-max-w="400" data-max-h="400" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></p
 icture><p><strong>The Antarctic Treaty\, Dual Use Technologies\, and the 
 Challenges of Non-Militarization</strong></p><p>  The Antarctic Treaty re
 presents one of the most enduring and successful arms control agreements 
 of the post–Second World War period. Concluded in 1959 at the height of
  early Cold War rivalry\, the Treaty pioneered peaceful use of an entire 
 continent\, by prohibiting military bases\, military maneuvers\, and weap
 ons testing - while permitting the use of military personnel and equipmen
 t for scientific or other peaceful purposes. Central to this achievement 
 is the non-militarization obligation in the Treaty's Article I\, which is
  reinforced by an open onsite and aerial inspection regime.</p><p>  This 
 seminar examines how contemporary technological developments are impactin
 g these foundations. It highlights the growing presence of space-related 
 infrastructure in Antarctica\, such as satellite ground receiving station
 s\, telescopes\, autonomous systems\, and their associated data networks.
  While these technologies are important for modern scientific research an
 d environmental monitoring\, many also possess inherent capabilities that
  could be incorporated into military systems operating either within or b
 eyond the Antarctic region. This raises new questions about how the Treat
 y's non-militarization obligations apply\, and how they can be modernized
  in circumstances where technologies being used in the region are inheren
 tly 'dual use'.</p><p><strong>Bio</strong></p><p>  Professor Jeff McGee i
 s focused on strategies that can help us govern Antarctica and the Southe
 rn Ocean. He is an expert in international law\, climate change law and g
 lobal environmental governance. Associate Professor McGee analyses the st
 rategies and structures of global and regional institutions that seek to 
 solve problems like climate change and the international laws that affect
  them. He provides critical analysis and commentary on the development an
 d policy of institutions such as the Antarctic Treaty System and United N
 ations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCC).<br /><br />  Profe
 ssor Jeff McGee focusses on international law and governance issues relat
 ing to Antarctica\, Oceans and Climate Change. As an international lawyer
  with expertise also in global governance and geopolitics\, he divides hi
 s time between the School of Law and the Institute for Marine and Antarct
 ic Studies. Jeff is also Director of the Australian Forum for Climate Int
 ervention Governance (AFCIG)\, the leading national research group on the
  governance of climate intervention technologies.</p>
