GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:EASC Colloquium: Adrift in the South - Xiao Hai\, Tony Hao
DESCRIPTION:When Xiao Hai turned fifteen\, his family paid a vocational-s
 chool teacher 1\,200 yuan to find him a factory job in Shenzhen. So began
  fifteen years spent moving between the garment mills and electronics fac
 tories of China's fast-growing southern cities.Adrift in the South is a m
 emoir of life as a migrant labourer in the twenty-first century\, making 
 iPhones and baby clothes\, hand-stitching football shirts and cutting pla
 stic into radios. Here\, Xiao Hai reveals the alienation and tedium of fa
 ctory life\, the small indignities and indifference of the larger system.
  And he tells the story of how poetry led him somewhere unexpected: to jo
 in a small community of artists living\, working\, and studying together 
 on the outskirts of Beijing.  This memoiris a landmark text from China's 
 migrant worker literature movement\, a grassroots group of writers provid
 ing an unvarnished account of what life is like for the 300 million migra
 nt workers powering the world's second-biggest economy.About the author: 
 Xiao Hai was born in Shangqiu City in Henan Province in China. At fifteen
  he moved to Shenzhen to join the workforce\, moving between cities and f
 actory jobs while writing poetry in his spare time. He is the author of t
 he poetry collection Sisyphus on the Wenyu River\, and translations of hi
 s poetry have appeared in World Literature Today and Chinese Literature T
 oday.About the translator: Tony Hao is a New Haven\, Connecticut-based Ch
 inese-to-English literary translator and writer. Authors he has translate
 d include Taiwanese novelist Tong Wei-Ger 童偉格\, Chinese worker-poet
  Xiao Hai 小海\, Taiwanese transgender writer Liu Chen-Chun 劉宸君\,
  and Chinese short story writer Ban Yu 班宇.  His debut book-length tra
 nslation\, Adrift in the South\, a memoir by Xiao Hai\, was published by 
 Granta Magazine Editions in May\, 2026.  His short-form translations have
  appeared in Granta\, The Wall Street Journal\, The Georgia Review\, The 
 Offing\, Books from Taiwan\, Florescence (The Taipei Chinese PEN)\, MAYDA
 Y Magazine\, and elsewhere. His work has been recognized by the PEN Trans
 lates Grant\, the Granum Foundation Translation Prize\, Art Omi Writers: 
 Translation Lab\, the Vermont Studio Center\, BCLT's Advanced Translation
  Workshop\, the Bread Loaf Translators' Conference\, and elsewhere.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  When Xiao Hai turned fifteen\, his fami
 ly paid a vocational-school teacher 1\,200 yuan to find him a factory job
  in Shenzhen. So began fifteen years spent moving between the garment mil
 ls and electronics factories of China's fast-growing southern cities.</p>
 <p><i>Adrift in the South </i>is a memoir of life as a migrant labourer i
 n the twenty-first century\, making iPhones and baby clothes\, hand-stitc
 hing football shirts and cutting plastic into radios. Here\, Xiao Hai rev
 eals the alienation and tedium of factory life\, the small indignities an
 d indifference of the larger system. And he tells the story of how poetry
  led him somewhere unexpected: to join a small community of artists livin
 g\, working\, and studying together on the outskirts of Beijing.</p><p>  
 This memoir<i></i>is a landmark text from China's migrant worker literatu
 re movement\, a grassroots group of writers providing an unvarnished acco
 unt of what life is like for the 300 million migrant workers powering the
  world's second-biggest economy.</p><p><strong>About the author:</strong>
  Xiao Hai was born in Shangqiu City in Henan Province in China. At fiftee
 n he moved to Shenzhen to join the workforce\, moving between cities and 
 factory jobs while writing poetry in his spare time. He is the author of 
 the poetry collection <i>Sisyphus on the Wenyu River</i>\, and translatio
 ns of his poetry have appeared in <i>World Literature Today</i> and <i>Ch
 inese Literature Today</i>.</p><p class="" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"
 ><strong>About the translator:</strong> Tony Hao is a New Haven\, Connect
 icut-based Chinese-to-English literary translator and writer. Authors he 
 has translated include Taiwanese novelist Tong Wei-Ger 童偉格\, Chines
 e worker-poet Xiao Hai 小海\, Taiwanese transgender writer Liu Chen-Chu
 n 劉宸君\, and Chinese short story writer Ban Yu 班宇.</p><p class="
 " data-rte-preserve-empty="true">  His debut book-length translation\, <e
 m>Adrift in the South</em>\, a memoir by Xiao Hai\, was published by Gran
 ta Magazine Editions in May\, 2026.</p><p class="" data-rte-preserve-empt
 y="true">  His short-form translations have appeared in <em>Granta</em>\,
  <em>The Wall Street Journal\, The Georgia Review\, The Offing</em>\, <em
 >Books from Taiwan\, Florescence (The Taipei Chinese PEN)\, MAYDAY Magazi
 ne</em>\, and elsewhere. His work has been recognized by the PEN Translat
 es Grant\, the Granum Foundation Translation Prize\, Art Omi Writers: Tra
 nslation Lab\, the Vermont Studio Center\, BCLT's Advanced Translation Wo
 rkshop\, the Bread Loaf Translators' Conference\, and elsewhere.</p>
