SUMMARY:ACES Horizon Talk: ""The Soviet Factor in China's Regional Ethnic
  Autonomy: A Sino-Soviet Comparison of Institutions and Ethnic Elites." b
 y Dr. Jun Kumakura
DESCRIPTION:Since the founding of the Chinese Communist Party in the 1920
 s\, the Party advocated the idea of a federal state under the influence o
 f the Soviet Union. In 1949\, however\, shortly before the founding of th
 e People's Republic of China\, the CCP turned toward a form of state-buil
 ding different from federalism and adopted regional ethnic autonomy. Whil
 e this shift is often understood as reflecting China's distinctive approa
 ch to state-building\, the Soviet dimension—including Stalin's apparent
  acceptance of the CCP's decision—has received comparatively little att
 ention.  Drawing on research developed from his doctoral dissertation\, P
 rofessor Jun Kumakura examines the Soviet influence on the CCP's system o
 f regional ethnic autonomy and traces the subsequent divergence between C
 hinese and Soviet policies toward ethnic elites.Date &amp\; Time:  Monday
 \, November 9  12:00 PM – 1:30 PMLocation:  GISB 1060This talk will be 
 held in person only. Lunch will be provided.  This event is sponsored by:
 Central Eurasian Studies (CEUS)The Inner Asian and Uralic National Resour
 ce Center (IAUNRC)The Byrnes Institute (REEI)The East Asian Studies Cente
 r (EASC)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Since the founding of the Chinese Commu
 nist Party in the 1920s\, the Party advocated the idea of a federal state
  under the influence of the Soviet Union. In 1949\, however\, shortly bef
 ore the founding of the People's Republic of China\, the CCP turned towar
 d a form of state-building different from federalism and adopted regional
  ethnic autonomy. While this shift is often understood as reflecting Chin
 a's distinctive approach to state-building\, the Soviet dimension—inclu
 ding Stalin's apparent acceptance of the CCP's decision—has received co
 mparatively little attention.</p><p>  Drawing on research developed from 
 his doctoral dissertation\, Professor Jun Kumakura examines the Soviet in
 fluence on the CCP's system of regional ethnic autonomy and traces the su
 bsequent divergence between Chinese and Soviet policies toward ethnic eli
 tes.</p><p><strong>Date &amp\; Time:</strong><br />  Monday\, November 9<
 br />  12:00 PM – 1:30 PM<br /><br /><strong>Location:</strong><br />  
 GISB 1060</p><p><strong>This talk will be held in person only. Lunch will
  be provided.</strong></p><p>  This event is sponsored by:</p><ul><li><em
 >Central Eurasian Studies (CEUS)</em></li><li><em>The Inner Asian and Ura
 lic National Resource Center (IAUNRC)</em></li><li><em>The Byrnes Institu
 te (REEI)</em></li><li><em>The East Asian Studies Center (EASC)</em></li>
 </ul>
