X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20270129T170000Z
DTEND:20270129T180000Z
LOCATION:355 North Eagleson Avenue
GEO:39.170011;-86.517418
SUMMARY:IKS Colloquium - Junyoung Verónica Kim
DESCRIPTION:IKS Spring 2027 Colloquium Series  Junyoung Verónica Kim  De
 partment of Asian and Asian American Studies  Binghamton University  Gwan
 gju from Buenos Aires: Missing Bodies and Forgotten Geographies in Im Heu
 ng-soon's Good Light\, Good Air&#160\;Friday\, January 2912:00-1:00pmGISB
  2067 | 355 N Eagleson Ave  On April 30th\, 1977\, fourteen mothers sent 
 a letter asking the military dictator Jorge Rafael Videla for the whereab
 outs of their disappeared children and began to gather in silent protest 
 in the Plaza de Mayo\, Buenos Aires. Known as the Mothers of Plaza de M
 ayo\, they spurred a mass movement that continues today. Around the same 
 time\, on May 18th\, 1980\, citizens of Gwangju\, South Korea\, launched 
 a mass resistance movement against the dictatorship of Chun Doo-hwan. The
  South Korean military retaliated in full force resulting in the massacre
  and disappearance of thousands of Gwangju citizens. The connections and 
 intimacies between the overlapping histories of these two cities demonstr
 ate the ways in which Global South countries were violently conscripted i
 nto dirty wars against communism. Despite the explicit linkages\, why are
  the histories of these two places (cities and nations) studied separatel
 y––divided by discrete disciplinary boundaries? How does insurgent me
 mory both disrupt dominant historical national narratives and yoke togeth
 er these two geographies/histories/futurities? Moreover\, how does the pr
 esent absence of the missing in Gwangju and the desaparecidos (disapp
 eared) in Buenos Aires haunt the urban geographies that are shaped throug
 h the ordinary violence of gentrification and urban development? Through 
 a reading of Im Heung-soon's video installation and documentary Good Ligh
 t\, Good Air (2018\, 2020)\, Junyoung Verónica Kim explores the multiple
  vectors and scales of transpacific connectivity. Examined through Eyal W
 eizman's conceptualization of forensic architecture\, the ruins\, holes\,
  and missing parts of both the material (bodies and urban geographies) an
 d the epistemic (archives\, knowledge production\, disciplinary formation
 ) testify to what cannot be visiblized or seen otherwise. In situating th
 e Gwangju Uprising and the Argentine dirty war as part and parcel of the 
 diasporic\, this talk proposes that paying attention to Korean-Latin Amer
 ican intimacies offers a possibility of fabulating a decolonial and demil
 itarized world.&#160\;  Open to the public. A light lunch will be served.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;">  IKS Sprin
 g 2027 Colloquium Series</h5><h6 style="text-align: center\;"><br />  Jun
 young Verónica Kim</h6><p style="text-align: center\;">  Department of A
 sian and Asian American Studies<br />  Binghamton University</p><p style=
 "text-align: center\;">  Gwangju from Buenos Aires: Missing Bodies and Fo
 rgotten Geographies in Im Heung-soon's <em>Good Light\, Good Air&#160\;</
 em></p><p style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Friday\, January 29</stron
 g></p><p style="text-align: center\;"><strong>12:00-1:00pm</strong></p><p
  style="text-align: center\;"><span class="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off f
 ont-feature-clig-off font-feature-calt-off text-decoration-none text-stri
 kethrough-none"><strong>GISB 2067 | 355 N Eagleson Ave</strong><br /><br 
 />  On April 30th\, 1977\, fourteen mothers sent a letter asking the mili
 tary dictator Jorge Rafael Videla for the whereabouts of their disappeare
 d children and began to gather in silent protest in the Plaza de Mayo\, B
 uenos Aires. Known as the Mothers of Plaza de Mayo\, they spurred a mas
 s movement that continues today. Around the same time\, on May 18th\, 198
 0\, citizens of Gwangju\, South Korea\, launched a mass resistance moveme
 nt against the dictatorship of Chun Doo-hwan. The South Korean military r
 etaliated in full force resulting in the massacre and disappearance of th
 ousands of Gwangju citizens. The connections and intimacies between the o
 verlapping histories of these two cities demonstrate the ways in which Gl
 obal South countries were violently conscripted into dirty wars against c
 ommunism. Despite the explicit linkages\, why are the histories of these 
 two places (cities and nations) studied separately––divided by discre
 te disciplinary boundaries? How does insurgent memory both disrupt domina
 nt historical national narratives and yoke together these two geographies
 /histories/futurities? Moreover\, how does the present absence of the mis
 sing in Gwangju and the desaparecidos (disappeared) in Buenos Aires h
 aunt the urban geographies that are shaped through the ordinary violence 
 of gentrification and urban development? Through a reading of Im Heung-so
 on's video installation and documentary Good Light\, Good Air (2018\, 202
 0)\, Junyoung Verónica Kim explores the multiple vectors and scales of t
 ranspacific connectivity. Examined through Eyal Weizman's conceptualizati
 on of forensic architecture\, the ruins\, holes\, and missing parts of bo
 th the material (bodies and urban geographies) and the epistemic (archive
 s\, knowledge production\, disciplinary formation) testify to what cannot
  be visiblized or seen otherwise. In situating the Gwangju Uprising and t
 he Argentine dirty war as part and parcel of the diasporic\, this talk pr
 oposes that paying attention to Korean-Latin American intimacies offers a
  possibility of fabulating a decolonial and demilitarized world.&#160\;<b
 r /><br />  Open to the public. A light lunch will be served.</span></p>
